3. Create the PostgreSQL database in Render

Open Render Dashboard

Step 3.1 — Create your Render account
1. Visit Render.
2. Select Get Started or Sign Up.
3. Continue using your GitHub account.
4. Authorize the repository access required for deployment.
5. Open the Render Dashboard.
Step 3.2 — Create a database
In Render:
1. Click New.
2. Select Postgres.
3. Fill in the database details.
Use the following configuration:
Field	Value
Name	helpdesk-ai-db
Database	helpdesk_ai
User	Render-generated owner
Region	Singapore
PostgreSQL Version	Supported default
Instance Type	Free

You can leave the database username and password generation to Render.

Select Create Database, confirming the plan shows Free before submitting.
Step 3.3 — Find your database URL
After the database becomes available:
1. Open helpdesk-ai-db.
2. Look for the connection details.
3. Locate External Database URL.
4. Use its copy control.
5. Store the URL privately.
The connection URL has a structure resembling:
postgresql://USERNAME:PASSWORD@HOSTNAME/DATABASE?sslmode=require


Your values will be different.
Important security requirement

The External Database URL initially uses an owner-level database account. You will use this only for initial database maintenance and provisioning, not as the final API runtime account.
Step 3.4 — Configure external database access
In the Render database page:
1. Find Access Control, Networking, or the external access configuration.
2. Identify your current public IPv4 address through a trusted IP lookup.
3. Add that address with a /32 prefix to the allowed range.
4. Save the rule.
Example format:
203.0.113.25/32


This is a format example, not your actual public IP.

Do not use 0.0.0.0/0 unless temporarily necessary for controlled troubleshooting; restrict database access to the required networks.

Render documents how its external database access rules work. 
Render Docs


4. Create the private database credentials file
Return to VS Code.
Step 4.1 — Open the server directory
cd .\helpdesk-ai\server


Step 4.2 — Create the security directory
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force .security


Step 4.3 — Create the owner environment file
Run:
notepad .security\render-owner.env


If Notepad asks to create the file, click Yes.
Step 4.4 — Enter your credentials
Paste this template and replace the values:
ADMIN_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://RENDER_OWNER:RENDER_PASSWORD@RENDER_HOST/helpdesk_ai?sslmode=require"

SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL="admin@example.com"
SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD="YOUR_NEW_STRONG_ADMIN_PASSWORD"

SEED_USER_EMAIL="demo@example.com"
SEED_USER_PASSWORD="YOUR_NEW_STRONG_DEMO_PASSWORD"


Use your actual Render External Database URL in the first value.

Important: Create two distinct, strong passwords for the seeded accounts. The admin and demo user are separate accounts.

Save the file using Ctrl + S, then close Notepad.

Do not upload this file to GitHub, Render, or Vercel. It remains on your development computer.

5. Install dependencies and initialize the cloud database
All commands in this section run from:
YOUR-REPOSITORY/helpdesk-ai/server


Step 5.1 — Install dependencies
npm.cmd ci


This installs the dependencies recorded in your package-lock.json.
Step 5.2 — Generate the Prisma client
npm.cmd run generate


This prepares your application's database client based on the Prisma schema.
Step 5.3 — Run production migrations
npm.cmd run migrate:deploy -- --env-file .security/render-owner.env


This should apply the existing database migrations to your new Render PostgreSQL database.
Step 5.4 — Create a restricted database runtime account
npm.cmd run security:provision-db -- --env-file .security/render-owner.env


Your deployment tooling is expected to create a restricted database account named helpdesk_runtime and write its connection string to:
helpdesk-ai/server/.security/cloud-runtime.env


Step 5.5 — Create initial admin and demo accounts
node scripts/seed-cloud.js --env-file .security/render-owner.env


This should create the initial administrator and demo user using the email addresses and passwords from your private file.

The deployment instructions indicate that those users must change their passwords after first login.
Step 5.6 — Find the restricted database URL
Open the generated file:
notepad .security\cloud-runtime.env


It should contain a DATABASE_URL value similar in structure to:
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://helpdesk_runtime:GENERATED_PASSWORD@RENDER_HOST/helpdesk_ai?sslmode=require"


Copy its actual value privately for the Render API configuration.

This is an illustrative connection string; your provisioning script determines the actual output.

Do not run the provisioning step repeatedly unless needed. According to your project instructions, running it again rotates the runtime account password, which also requires updating Render.

If PostgreSQL refuses role creation, do not substitute the database owner account as the API runtime user. Your current application intentionally rejects privileged credentials.

